/* Country Flags subject for Trivia 2032.
   Country data: world-countries (mledoze, ODbL). Facts and questions: hand-written. */
window.TRIVIA_DATA=window.TRIVIA_DATA||{};
window.TRIVIA_DATA.flags={"countries":[{"c":"AF","n":"Afghanistan","cap":"Kabul","ct":"Asia","sub":"Southern Asia","ll":[33,65],"id":"004","t":4,"f":"This black, red and green tricolour with a white mosque emblem was the flag of the Islamic Republic of Afghanistan. Since 2021 the Taliban government has flown a white flag with the Islamic declaration of faith instead, so Afghanistan's flag is disputed."},{"c":"AO","n":"Angola","cap":"Luanda","ct":"Africa","sub":"Middle Africa","ll":[-12.5,18.5],"id":"024","t":4,"f":"Red over black with a yellow half cog-wheel, machete and star. The cog stands for industrial workers, the machete for farmers, and the star for progress; red recalls the independence struggle and black stands for Africa."},{"c":"AL","n":"Albania","cap":"Tirana","ct":"Europe","sub":"Southeast Europe","ll":[41,20],"id":"008","t":4,"f":"A black double-headed eagle on red. The eagle was the symbol of Skanderbeg, the 15th-century hero who led Albania's resistance against the Ottoman Empire."},{"c":"AD","n":"Andorra","cap":"Andorra la Vella","ct":"Europe","sub":"Southern Europe","ll":[42.5,1.5],"id":"020","t":5,"f":"Blue, yellow and red stripes with a coat of arms. The colours nod to France and Catalonia (Spain), because Andorra is jointly headed by two co-princes: the President of France and the Bishop of Urgell."},{"c":"AE","n":"United Arab Emirates","cap":"Abu Dhabi","ct":"Asia","sub":"Western Asia","ll":[24,54],"id":"784","t":3,"f":"Green, white and black stripes with a red band at the pole — the Pan-Arab colours shared by many Arab nations."},{"c":"AR","n":"Argentina","cap":"Buenos Aires","ct":"South America","sub":"South America","ll":[-34,-64],"id":"032","t":1,"f":"Light blue and white stripes with the golden 'Sun of May'. The sun honours the May Revolution of 1810 that began Argentina's path to independence from Spain."},{"c":"AM","n":"Armenia","cap":"Yerevan","ct":"Asia","sub":"Western Asia","tc":true,"ll":[40,45],"id":"051","t":4,"f":"Red, blue and orange stripes. Red recalls the Armenian people's struggle for survival, blue the peaceful sky, and orange (apricot) their courage and hard work."},{"c":"AG","n":"Antigua and Barbuda","cap":"Saint John's","ct":"North America","sub":"Caribbean","ll":[17.05,-61.8],"id":"028","t":5,"f":"A rising golden sun over black, blue and white bands inside a red 'V'. The sun marks the dawn of a new era, black the islanders' African heritage, blue the sea, and the V shape stands for victory."},{"c":"AU","n":"Australia","cap":"Canberra","ct":"Oceania","sub":"Australia and New Zealand","ll":[-27,133],"id":"036","t":1,"f":"The Union Jack recalls Australia's history as a British colony, the big seven-pointed Commonwealth Star stands for its states and territories, and the five stars on the right form the Southern Cross constellation, seen in southern skies."},{"c":"AT","n":"Austria","cap":"Vienna","ct":"Europe","sub":"Central Europe","ll":[47.33,13.33],"id":"040","t":2,"f":"Red-white-red is one of the oldest national flags. Legend says Duke Leopold V's white tunic was soaked in blood after a battle in 1191 — except for a white band where his belt had been."},{"c":"AZ","n":"Azerbaijan","cap":"Baku","ct":"Asia","sub":"Western Asia","tc":true,"ll":[40.5,47.5],"id":"031","t":4,"f":"Blue, red and green stripes with a white crescent and eight-pointed star. Blue stands for Turkic heritage, red for progress and modern ideas, and green for Islam."},{"c":"BI","n":"Burundi","cap":"Gitega","ct":"Africa","sub":"Eastern Africa","ll":[-3.5,30],"id":"108","t":5,"f":"A white cross divides red and green, with three red stars in a white circle. The stars stand for Burundi's three main peoples and its motto: Unity, Work, Progress."},{"c":"BE","n":"Belgium","cap":"Brussels","ct":"Europe","sub":"Western Europe","ll":[50.83,4],"id":"056","t":2,"f":"Black, yellow and red vertical stripes, taken from the coat of arms of the old Duchy of Brabant: a golden lion with red claws on black. Don't confuse it with Germany's horizontal black, red and gold."},{"c":"BJ","n":"Benin","cap":"Porto-Novo","ct":"Africa","sub":"Western Africa","ll":[9.5,2.25],"id":"204","t":5,"f":"A green vertical band next to yellow over red. Green, yellow and red are the 'Pan-African colours' used by many African countries to show unity and independence."},{"c":"BF","n":"Burkina Faso","cap":"Ouagadougou","ct":"Africa","sub":"Western Africa","ll":[13,-2],"id":"854","t":5,"f":"Red over green with a yellow star. Red recalls the revolution, green the country's farmland and hope, and the star is a guiding light for the future."},{"c":"BD","n":"Bangladesh","cap":"Dhaka","ct":"Asia","sub":"Southern Asia","ll":[24,90],"id":"050","t":4,"f":"A red disc on dark green. The red stands for the sun rising over Bengal and for those who died in the 1971 Liberation War; green is the country's lush land. The disc sits slightly towards the pole so it looks centred when the flag flies."},{"c":"BG","n":"Bulgaria","cap":"Sofia","ct":"Europe","sub":"Southeast Europe","ll":[43,25],"id":"100","t":4,"f":"White, green and red stripes. White stands for peace, green for Bulgaria's farmland, and red for the courage of its people."},{"c":"BH","n":"Bahrain","cap":"Manama","ct":"Asia","sub":"Western Asia","ll":[26,50.55],"id":"048","t":5,"f":"Red with a white zig-zag band that has five points. The five points stand for the Five Pillars of Islam. It looks a lot like Qatar's flag, which is maroon and has nine points."},{"c":"BS","n":"Bahamas","cap":"Nassau","ct":"North America","sub":"Caribbean","ll":[24.25,-76],"id":"044","t":4,"f":"A black triangle points into aquamarine and gold stripes. The aquamarine is the sea around the islands, gold is the sun and sandy beaches, and black shows the strength and unity of the Bahamian people."},{"c":"BA","n":"Bosnia and Herzegovina","cap":"Sarajevo","ct":"Europe","sub":"Southeast Europe","ll":[44,18],"id":"070","t":4,"f":"A yellow triangle and a line of white stars on blue. The triangle roughly matches the shape of the country and its three corners stand for its three main peoples: Bosniaks, Croats and Serbs. The stars stand for Europe."},{"c":"BY","n":"Belarus","cap":"Minsk","ct":"Europe","sub":"Eastern Europe","ll":[53,28],"id":"112","t":4,"f":"Red over green with a red-and-white folk ornament pattern at the pole. The pattern is a traditional Belarusian embroidery design, like those woven into towels and clothing."},{"c":"BZ","n":"Belize","cap":"Belmopan","ct":"North America","sub":"Central America","ll":[17.25,-88.75],"id":"084","t":5,"f":"Blue with red stripes and a coat of arms showing two woodcutters beside a mahogany tree, because logging built the colony. It's one of the very few national flags that show people."},{"c":"BO","n":"Bolivia","cap":"Sucre","ct":"South America","sub":"South America","ll":[-17,-65],"id":"068","t":3,"f":"Red, yellow and green stripes with a coat of arms. Red stands for the heroes who fought for independence, yellow for Bolivia's rich minerals, and green for its fertile land."},{"c":"BR","n":"Brazil","cap":"Brasília","ct":"South America","sub":"South America","ll":[-10,-55],"id":"076","t":1,"f":"A yellow diamond on green with a blue globe. The globe shows the night sky over Rio de Janeiro on 15 November 1889, the day Brazil became a republic, with one star for each state and the capital district. The motto reads 'Ordem e Progresso' — Order and Progress."},{"c":"BB","n":"Barbados","cap":"Bridgetown","ct":"North America","sub":"Caribbean","ll":[13.17,-59.53],"id":"052","t":5,"f":"Blue, gold and blue bands with a black trident head. The blue is the ocean and sky, gold the sand, and the trident comes from the sea god Neptune — its broken shaft shows Barbados breaking away from its colonial past."},{"c":"BN","n":"Brunei","cap":"Bandar Seri Begawan","ct":"Asia","sub":"South-Eastern Asia","ll":[4.5,114.67],"id":"096","t":5,"f":"Yellow with slanting white and black bands and a red crest. Yellow is the colour of the Sultan, and the crest includes an umbrella (royalty), a crescent (Islam) and two raised hands (the government's care for the people)."},{"c":"BT","n":"Bhutan","cap":"Thimphu","ct":"Asia","sub":"Southern Asia","ll":[27.5,90.5],"id":"064","t":4,"f":"A white Druk, the 'Thunder Dragon', across yellow and orange. Yellow stands for the king's authority and orange for Buddhism. Bhutan's own name for itself, Druk Yul, means 'Land of the Thunder Dragon'."},{"c":"BW","n":"Botswana","cap":"Gaborone","ct":"Africa","sub":"Southern Africa","ll":[-22,24],"id":"072","t":5,"f":"Light blue with a black stripe edged in white. The blue stands for water and rain ('Pula', which is also the currency's name), and the black and white stand for people of different races living in harmony, like a zebra's stripes."},{"c":"CF","n":"Central African Republic","cap":"Bangui","ct":"Africa","sub":"Middle Africa","ll":[7,21],"id":"140","t":5,"f":"Four horizontal stripes crossed by a red vertical band, with a yellow star. It mixes the Pan-African colours with France's blue, white and red, and the red band stands for the blood that unites all people."},{"c":"CA","n":"Canada","cap":"Ottawa","ct":"North America","sub":"North America","ll":[60,-95],"id":"124","t":1,"f":"A red maple leaf between two red bands. Maple trees grow across Canada and the leaf has been a Canadian symbol for over 200 years. The flag was adopted in 1965."},{"c":"CH","n":"Switzerland","cap":"Bern","ct":"Europe","sub":"Western Europe","ll":[47,8],"id":"756","t":1,"f":"A white cross on a red square. It's one of only two square national flags (with Vatican City). The Red Cross emblem reverses these colours to honour Switzerland, where the organisation was founded."},{"c":"CL","n":"Chile","cap":"Santiago","ct":"South America","sub":"South America","ll":[-30,-71],"id":"152","t":2,"f":"A white star on a blue square, with white and red stripes. The star is a guide to progress and honour, blue is the sky and Pacific Ocean, white the snowy Andes mountains, and red the blood of independence heroes."},{"c":"CN","n":"China","cap":"Beijing","ct":"Asia","sub":"Eastern Asia","ll":[35,105],"id":"156","t":1,"f":"Red with five yellow stars. The big star stands for the Communist Party of China and the four smaller stars circling it stand for the people; red is the colour of the revolution."},{"c":"CI","n":"Ivory Coast","cap":"Yamoussoukro","ct":"Africa","sub":"Western Africa","ll":[8,-5],"id":"384","t":4,"f":"Orange, white and green vertical stripes: orange for the savannah, white for peace, green for hope and the forests. It's a mirror image of Ireland's flag, which has green next to the pole."},{"c":"CM","n":"Cameroon","cap":"Yaoundé","ct":"Africa","sub":"Middle Africa","ll":[6,12],"id":"120","t":4,"f":"Green, red and yellow stripes with a yellow star. The star is the 'star of unity'; green stands for the southern forests, yellow for the northern savannah, and red for unity between them."},{"c":"CD","n":"DR Congo","cap":"Kinshasa","ct":"Africa","sub":"Middle Africa","ll":[0,25],"id":"180","t":3,"f":"Sky blue with a red diagonal band edged in yellow, and a yellow star. Blue stands for peace, red for the blood of the country's martyrs, yellow for its wealth, and the star for a radiant future."},{"c":"CG","n":"Republic of the Congo","cap":"Brazzaville","ct":"Africa","sub":"Middle Africa","ll":[-1,15],"id":"178","t":5,"f":"A yellow band runs diagonally between green and red, the Pan-African colours. Green stands for the forests, yellow for friendship and the nation's wealth, and red for the struggle for independence."},{"c":"CO","n":"Colombia","cap":"Bogotá","ct":"South America","sub":"South America","ll":[4,-72],"id":"170","t":2,"f":"Yellow, blue and red stripes, with the yellow twice as wide. Yellow is the country's wealth, blue its two coasts, and red the blood of independence fighters. Ecuador and Venezuela have similar flags because all three were once part of 'Gran Colombia'."},{"c":"KM","n":"Comoros","cap":"Moroni","ct":"Africa","sub":"Eastern Africa","ll":[-12.17,44.25],"id":"174","t":5,"f":"Four stripes and a green triangle with a white crescent and four stars. The four stars and four stripes stand for the four main islands of the Comoros archipelago."},{"c":"CV","n":"Cape Verde","cap":"Praia","ct":"Africa","sub":"Western Africa","ll":[16,-24],"id":"132","t":5,"f":"Blue with white and red stripes and a ring of ten yellow stars. The ten stars stand for the ten main islands of Cape Verde, and the blue for the Atlantic Ocean around them."},{"c":"CR","n":"Costa Rica","cap":"San José","ct":"North America","sub":"Central America","ll":[10,-84],"id":"188","t":4,"f":"Blue, white, red, white and blue stripes. The design was inspired by France's blue, white and red, the colours of liberty after the French Revolution."},{"c":"CU","n":"Cuba","cap":"Havana","ct":"North America","sub":"Caribbean","ll":[21.5,-80],"id":"192","t":2,"f":"Five blue and white stripes with a red triangle and a white 'lone star'. The three blue stripes stand for Cuba's old regions, the red for blood shed in the fight for independence, and the star for freedom."},{"c":"CY","n":"Cyprus","cap":"Nicosia","ct":"Europe","sub":"Southern Europe","tc":true,"ll":[35,33],"id":"196","t":3,"f":"White with a copper-coloured map of the island and two olive branches. The copper colour recalls the island's ancient copper mines, and the olive branches stand for peace between Greek and Turkish Cypriots."},{"c":"CZ","n":"Czechia","cap":"Prague","ct":"Europe","sub":"Central Europe","ll":[49.75,15.5],"id":"203","t":3,"f":"White over red with a blue triangle at the pole. The Czech Republic kept this flag after it peacefully split from Slovakia in 1993."},{"c":"DE","n":"Germany","cap":"Berlin","ct":"Europe","sub":"Western Europe","ll":[51,9],"id":"276","t":1,"f":"Black, red and gold stripes. The colours come from the uniforms of volunteers who fought Napoleon in the early 1800s and became a symbol of unity and democracy."},{"c":"DJ","n":"Djibouti","cap":"Djibouti","ct":"Africa","sub":"Eastern Africa","ll":[11.5,43],"id":"262","t":5,"f":"Light blue over green with a white triangle and a red star. Blue stands for the Issa people and green for the Afar people, the country's two main groups; white is for peace and the red star for unity."},{"c":"DM","n":"Dominica","cap":"Roseau","ct":"North America","sub":"Caribbean","ll":[15.42,-61.33],"id":"212","t":5,"f":"A green field with a yellow, black and white cross and a red disc holding a Sisserou parrot, the national bird. The ten green stars stand for Dominica's ten parishes. The parrot's feathers make it one of only two national flags with purple."},{"c":"DK","n":"Denmark","cap":"Copenhagen","ct":"Europe","sub":"Northern Europe","ll":[56,10],"id":"208","t":2,"f":"A white cross on red. Called the Dannebrog, it's claimed to be the oldest national flag still in use — legend says it fell from the sky during a battle in 1219. Its off-centre 'Nordic cross' inspired the other Nordic flags."},{"c":"DO","n":"Dominican Republic","cap":"Santo Domingo","ct":"North America","sub":"Caribbean","ll":[19,-70.67],"id":"214","t":4,"f":"A white cross divides blue and red squares, with a coat of arms in the centre. The coat of arms contains an open Bible — the only national flag to show one."},{"c":"DZ","n":"Algeria","cap":"Algiers","ct":"Africa","sub":"Northern Africa","ll":[28,3],"id":"012","t":4,"f":"Green and white halves with a red crescent and star. The crescent and star are symbols of Islam, and the red remembers those who died in the war of independence from France (1954–62)."},{"c":"EC","n":"Ecuador","cap":"Quito","ct":"South America","sub":"South America","ll":[-2,-77.5],"id":"218","t":3,"f":"Yellow, blue and red like Colombia's flag, but with a coat of arms showing a condor, the snow-capped Chimborazo volcano and the Guayas river."},{"c":"EG","n":"Egypt","cap":"Cairo","ct":"Africa","sub":"Northern Africa","tc":true,"ll":[27,30],"id":"818","t":1,"f":"Red, white and black stripes with a golden Eagle of Saladin. Red stands for the struggle against colonial rule, white for the bloodless 1952 revolution, and black for the end of oppression."},{"c":"ER","n":"Eritrea","cap":"Asmara","ct":"Africa","sub":"Eastern Africa","ll":[15,39],"id":"232","t":5,"f":"A red triangle between green and blue triangles, with a gold olive wreath and branch. Green stands for farming, blue for the Red Sea coast, red for the independence struggle, and the olive for peace."},{"c":"ES","n":"Spain","cap":"Madrid","ct":"Europe","sub":"Southern Europe","ll":[40,-4],"id":"724","t":1,"f":"Red, yellow and red stripes with a coat of arms. The coat of arms shows the Pillars of Hercules with the motto 'Plus Ultra', meaning 'Further beyond' — a nod to Spain's voyages to the Americas."},{"c":"EE","n":"Estonia","cap":"Tallinn","ct":"Europe","sub":"Northern Europe","ll":[59,26],"id":"233","t":3,"f":"Blue, black and white stripes. Blue stands for the sky and loyalty, black for the soil and Estonia's dark past, and white for snow, hope and a bright future."},{"c":"ET","n":"Ethiopia","cap":"Addis Ababa","ct":"Africa","sub":"Eastern Africa","ll":[8,38],"id":"231","t":3,"f":"Green, yellow and red with a blue disc and a golden star. Because Ethiopia was never colonised (apart from a brief Italian occupation from 1936 to 1941), these colours inspired the 'Pan-African colours' used on many African flags."},{"c":"FI","n":"Finland","cap":"Helsinki","ct":"Europe","sub":"Northern Europe","ll":[64,26],"id":"246","t":2,"f":"A blue cross on white. Blue stands for Finland's thousands of lakes and its sky, and white for the snow that covers the land in winter."},{"c":"FJ","n":"Fiji","cap":"Suva","ct":"Oceania","sub":"Melanesia","ll":[-18,175],"id":"242","t":4,"f":"Light blue with the Union Jack and a shield. The shield shows a British lion holding a cocoa pod, plus sugar cane, a coconut palm, bananas and a dove of peace — Fiji's crops and its hope for peace."},{"c":"FR","n":"France","cap":"Paris","ct":"Europe","sub":"Western Europe","ll":[46,2],"id":"250","t":1,"f":"Blue, white and red vertical stripes, known as the 'Tricolore'. It comes from the French Revolution: blue and red were the colours of Paris, and white was the colour of the king."},{"c":"FM","n":"Micronesia","cap":"Palikir","ct":"Oceania","sub":"Micronesia","ll":[6.92,158.25],"id":"583","t":5,"f":"Light blue with four white stars. Each star stands for one of the four states of Micronesia: Chuuk, Kosrae, Pohnpei and Yap."},{"c":"GA","n":"Gabon","cap":"Libreville","ct":"Africa","sub":"Middle Africa","ll":[-1,11.75],"id":"266","t":5,"f":"Green, yellow and blue stripes. Green stands for Gabon's rainforests, yellow for the sun and the equator running through the country, and blue for the sea."},{"c":"GB","n":"United Kingdom","cap":"London","ct":"Europe","sub":"Northern Europe","ll":[54,-2],"id":"826","t":1,"f":"The 'Union Jack' combines the red cross of Saint George (England), the white diagonal cross of Saint Andrew (Scotland) and the red diagonal cross of Saint Patrick (Ireland). Wales isn't shown because it was already united with England."},{"c":"GE","n":"Georgia","cap":"Tbilisi","ct":"Asia","sub":"Western Asia","tc":true,"ll":[42,43.5],"id":"268","t":4,"f":"A big red cross with four smaller red crosses on white, known as the 'Five Cross Flag'. The design goes back to medieval Georgia and the cross of Saint George."},{"c":"GH","n":"Ghana","cap":"Accra","ct":"Africa","sub":"Western Africa","ll":[8,-2],"id":"288","t":2,"f":"Red, gold and green with a black star. Ghana was one of the first colonies in sub-Saharan Africa to win independence (1957); the black star stands for African freedom, red for those who fought for it, gold for mineral wealth and green for forests."},{"c":"GN","n":"Guinea","cap":"Conakry","ct":"Africa","sub":"Western Africa","ll":[11,-10],"id":"324","t":5,"f":"Red, yellow and green vertical stripes: red for sacrifice, yellow for the sun and wealth, green for the land. It's the reverse order of Mali's flag."},{"c":"GM","n":"Gambia","cap":"Banjul","ct":"Africa","sub":"Western Africa","ll":[13.47,-16.57],"id":"270","t":5,"f":"Red, blue and green stripes with thin white edges. The blue stripe in the middle is the Gambia River, which flows through the whole length of this narrow country."},{"c":"GW","n":"Guinea-Bissau","cap":"Bissau","ct":"Africa","sub":"Western Africa","ll":[12,-15],"id":"624","t":5,"f":"A red vertical band with a black star, next to yellow over green. It uses the Pan-African colours and was inspired by Ghana's flag."},{"c":"GQ","n":"Equatorial Guinea","cap":"Malabo","ct":"Africa","sub":"Middle Africa","ll":[2,10],"id":"226","t":5,"f":"Green, white and red stripes, a blue triangle, and a coat of arms with a silk-cotton tree. The six stars stand for the mainland and the country's five islands."},{"c":"GR","n":"Greece","cap":"Athens","ct":"Europe","sub":"Southern Europe","ll":[39,22],"id":"300","t":1,"f":"Nine blue and white stripes with a white cross. The stripes are said to stand for the nine syllables of the Greek motto 'Eleftheria i Thanatos' — Freedom or Death — and the cross for the Greek Orthodox Church."},{"c":"GD","n":"Grenada","cap":"St. George's","ct":"North America","sub":"Caribbean","ll":[12.12,-61.67],"id":"308","t":5,"f":"A red border with six yellow stars, yellow and green triangles, a star in a red disc, and a nutmeg. Grenada is famous for growing nutmeg, and its seven stars stand for its seven parishes."},{"c":"GT","n":"Guatemala","cap":"Guatemala City","ct":"North America","sub":"Central America","ll":[15.5,-90.25],"id":"320","t":4,"f":"Sky blue, white and sky blue with a coat of arms. It shows a quetzal, the national bird, perched on a scroll with the date of independence: 15 September 1821."},{"c":"GY","n":"Guyana","cap":"Georgetown","ct":"South America","sub":"South America","ll":[5,-59],"id":"328","t":5,"f":"Nicknamed 'the Golden Arrowhead': a yellow arrowhead and red triangle on green. Green stands for the forests, yellow for mineral wealth, and red for the energy of building the nation."},{"c":"HN","n":"Honduras","cap":"Tegucigalpa","ct":"North America","sub":"Central America","ll":[15,-86.5],"id":"340","t":4,"f":"Blue, white and blue with five blue stars. The stars stand for the five nations of the old Federal Republic of Central America and the hope that they might unite again."},{"c":"HR","n":"Croatia","cap":"Zagreb","ct":"Europe","sub":"Southeast Europe","ll":[45.17,15.5],"id":"191","t":2,"f":"Red, white and blue with the red-and-white checkerboard shield called the 'šahovnica', a Croatian symbol for centuries. The crown on the shield is made of five small coats of arms of Croatia's historic regions."},{"c":"HT","n":"Haiti","cap":"Port-au-Prince","ct":"North America","sub":"Caribbean","ll":[19,-72.42],"id":"332","t":4,"f":"Blue over red with a coat of arms. It dates from 1803, when Haitian revolutionaries tore the white stripe out of the French flag to show they were free of France."},{"c":"HU","n":"Hungary","cap":"Budapest","ct":"Europe","sub":"Central Europe","ll":[47,20],"id":"348","t":3,"f":"Red, white and green stripes. Red stands for strength, white for faithfulness, and green for hope."},{"c":"ID","n":"Indonesia","cap":"Jakarta","ct":"Asia","sub":"South-Eastern Asia","ll":[-5,120],"id":"360","t":2,"f":"Red over white, called 'Sang Saka Merah-Putih'. Red stands for courage and white for purity. It's almost identical to Monaco's flag — only the shape is different."},{"c":"IN","n":"India","cap":"New Delhi","ct":"Asia","sub":"Southern Asia","ll":[20,77],"id":"356","t":1,"f":"Saffron, white and green with a navy-blue wheel, the Ashoka Chakra. The wheel has 24 spokes and comes from an ancient carving by Emperor Ashoka. Saffron stands for courage, white for peace and truth, and green for faith and the land."},{"c":"IE","n":"Ireland","cap":"Dublin","ct":"Europe","sub":"Northern Europe","ll":[53,-8],"id":"372","t":1,"f":"Green, white and orange. Green stands for Ireland's Gaelic tradition, orange for supporters of William of Orange, and white for peace between them."},{"c":"IR","n":"Iran","cap":"Tehran","ct":"Asia","sub":"Southern Asia","ll":[32,53],"id":"364","t":3,"f":"Green, white and red with a red emblem. Along the edges of the stripes, 'God is great' is written 22 times, marking the date the 1979 revolution succeeded: the 22nd of the month of Bahman."},{"c":"IQ","n":"Iraq","cap":"Baghdad","ct":"Asia","sub":"Western Asia","ll":[33,44],"id":"368","t":3,"f":"Red, white and black stripes with the words 'God is great' (Allahu Akbar) written in green Arabic script."},{"c":"IS","n":"Iceland","cap":"Reykjavik","ct":"Europe","sub":"Northern Europe","ll":[65,-18],"id":"352","t":2,"f":"A red cross outlined in white on blue. Blue is the ocean and sky, white is Iceland's ice and snow, and red is the fire of its volcanoes."},{"c":"IL","n":"Israel","cap":"Jerusalem","ct":"Asia","sub":"Western Asia","ll":[31.47,35.13],"id":"376","t":2,"f":"Two blue stripes and a blue Star of David on white. The stripes are inspired by the tallit, a Jewish prayer shawl, and the Star of David is a symbol of Judaism."},{"c":"IT","n":"Italy","cap":"Rome","ct":"Europe","sub":"Southern Europe","ll":[42.83,12.83],"id":"380","t":1,"f":"Green, white and red, known as 'Il Tricolore'. It was inspired by the French flag when Napoleon's armies came to Italy in the 1790s."},{"c":"JM","n":"Jamaica","cap":"Kingston","ct":"North America","sub":"Caribbean","ll":[18.25,-77.5],"id":"388","t":1,"f":"A gold diagonal cross with green and black triangles. Gold stands for sunshine and natural wealth, green for the land, and black for the strength of the people. It's the only national flag with no red, white or blue."},{"c":"JO","n":"Jordan","cap":"Amman","ct":"Asia","sub":"Western Asia","ll":[31,36],"id":"400","t":4,"f":"Black, white and green stripes with a red triangle and a white seven-pointed star. The seven points stand for the seven verses of the first chapter of the Quran."},{"c":"JP","n":"Japan","cap":"Tokyo","ct":"Asia","sub":"Eastern Asia","ll":[36,138],"id":"392","t":1,"f":"A red disc on white, called the 'Hinomaru' — the circle of the sun. Japan's name, Nippon, means 'origin of the sun', which is why it's called the Land of the Rising Sun."},{"c":"KZ","n":"Kazakhstan","cap":"Astana","ct":"Asia","sub":"Central Asia","tc":true,"ll":[48,68],"id":"398","t":3,"f":"Sky blue with a golden sun and a soaring steppe eagle. The blue is the endless sky over the steppe, and the ornament at the pole is a traditional Kazakh pattern."},{"c":"KE","n":"Kenya","cap":"Nairobi","ct":"Africa","sub":"Eastern Africa","ll":[1,38],"id":"404","t":2,"f":"Black, red and green stripes edged in white, with a Maasai shield and two spears. Black stands for the people, red for the struggle for independence, green for the land, white for peace, and the shield for defending freedom."},{"c":"KG","n":"Kyrgyzstan","cap":"Bishkek","ct":"Asia","sub":"Central Asia","ll":[41,75],"id":"417","t":5,"f":"Red with a yellow sun with 40 rays. The rays stand for the 40 tribes united by the legendary hero Manas, and the lines in the middle show the roof of a yurt, the traditional nomad tent."},{"c":"KH","n":"Cambodia","cap":"Phnom Penh","ct":"Asia","sub":"South-Eastern Asia","ll":[13,105],"id":"116","t":3,"f":"Blue, red and blue bands with Angkor Wat, a huge 12th-century temple, in white. It's one of the only national flags with a building on it."},{"c":"KI","n":"Kiribati","cap":"South Tarawa","ct":"Oceania","sub":"Micronesia","ll":[1.42,173],"id":"296","t":5,"f":"A frigatebird flies over a golden sun rising from the Pacific Ocean. The white and blue wavy lines are the ocean around Kiribati's islands."},{"c":"KN","n":"Saint Kitts and Nevis","cap":"Basseterre","ct":"North America","sub":"Caribbean","ll":[17.33,-62.75],"id":"659","t":5,"f":"A black diagonal band edged in yellow with two white stars, between green and red triangles. The two stars stand for hope and liberty."},{"c":"KR","n":"South Korea","cap":"Seoul","ct":"Asia","sub":"Eastern Asia","ll":[37,127.5],"id":"410","t":1,"f":"White with a red and blue yin-yang circle and four black trigrams, called the 'Taegukgi'. The circle stands for balance in the universe and the trigrams for heaven, earth, water and fire."},{"c":"KW","n":"Kuwait","cap":"Kuwait City","ct":"Asia","sub":"Western Asia","ll":[29.5,45.75],"id":"414","t":4,"f":"Green, white and red stripes with a black trapezoid at the pole — the 'Pan-Arab colours' shared by many Arab countries."},{"c":"LA","n":"Laos","cap":"Vientiane","ct":"Asia","sub":"South-Eastern Asia","ll":[18,105],"id":"418","t":4,"f":"Red, blue and red bands with a white disc. The disc is often said to be the full moon rising over the Mekong River, and the red stands for the blood shed for independence."},{"c":"LB","n":"Lebanon","cap":"Beirut","ct":"Asia","sub":"Western Asia","ll":[33.83,35.83],"id":"422","t":2,"f":"Red, white and red with a green cedar tree. The cedar of Lebanon is a symbol of strength, holiness and eternity; it's even mentioned in the Bible."},{"c":"LR","n":"Liberia","cap":"Monrovia","ct":"Africa","sub":"Western Africa","ll":[6.5,-9.5],"id":"430","t":4,"f":"Eleven red and white stripes and a white star on blue. Liberia was founded by freed African-Americans, so its flag is modelled on the US flag; the 11 stripes stand for the 11 people who signed its Declaration of Independence."},{"c":"LY","n":"Libya","cap":"Tripoli","ct":"Africa","sub":"Northern Africa","ll":[25,17],"id":"434","t":4,"f":"Red, black and green stripes with a white crescent and star. This was the flag of the Kingdom of Libya, brought back in 2011."},{"c":"LC","n":"Saint Lucia","cap":"Castries","ct":"North America","sub":"Caribbean","ll":[13.88,-60.97],"id":"662","t":5,"f":"A golden triangle in front of a black and white triangle on blue. The triangles are the Pitons, Saint Lucia's twin volcanic peaks rising from the blue Caribbean Sea."},{"c":"LI","n":"Liechtenstein","cap":"Vaduz","ct":"Europe","sub":"Western Europe","ll":[47.27,9.53],"id":"438","t":5,"f":"Blue over red with a golden crown. The crown was added in 1937 after Liechtenstein discovered at the 1936 Olympics that its flag was identical to Haiti's!"},{"c":"LK","n":"Sri Lanka","cap":"Colombo","ct":"Asia","sub":"Southern Asia","ll":[7,81],"id":"144","t":3,"f":"A golden lion holding a sword, with green and orange stripes. The lion stands for the Sinhalese people, the stripes for the Tamil and Muslim communities, and the leaves in the corners for Buddhism."},{"c":"LS","n":"Lesotho","cap":"Maseru","ct":"Africa","sub":"Southern Africa","ll":[-29.5,28.5],"id":"426","t":5,"f":"Blue, white and green stripes with a black 'mokorotlo', the traditional cone-shaped straw hat of the Basotho people."},{"c":"LT","n":"Lithuania","cap":"Vilnius","ct":"Europe","sub":"Northern Europe","ll":[56,24],"id":"440","t":3,"f":"Yellow, green and red stripes. Yellow stands for the sun and prosperity, green for forests and hope, and red for courage and the blood shed for freedom."},{"c":"LU","n":"Luxembourg","cap":"Luxembourg","ct":"Europe","sub":"Western Europe","ll":[49.75,6.17],"id":"442","t":3,"f":"Red, white and light blue stripes. It looks like the Netherlands' flag, but Luxembourg's is longer and its blue is lighter."},{"c":"LV","n":"Latvia","cap":"Riga","ct":"Europe","sub":"Northern Europe","ll":[57,25],"id":"428","t":3,"f":"Dark carmine red with a thin white stripe, one of the oldest flag designs in the world. Legend says a wounded leader was wrapped in a white sheet, which was stained red on both edges."},{"c":"MA","n":"Morocco","cap":"Rabat","ct":"Africa","sub":"Northern Africa","ll":[32,-5],"id":"504","t":2,"f":"Red with a green five-pointed star outline, called the Seal of Solomon. Red has been Morocco's colour for centuries, and the star's five points are often linked to the Five Pillars of Islam."},{"c":"MC","n":"Monaco","cap":"Monaco","ct":"Europe","sub":"Western Europe","ll":[43.73,7.4],"id":"492","t":3,"f":"Red over white, the heraldic colours of Monaco's ruling Grimaldi family for centuries. It's almost identical to Indonesia's flag, but squarer."},{"c":"MD","n":"Moldova","cap":"Chișinău","ct":"Europe","sub":"Eastern Europe","ll":[47,29],"id":"498","t":4,"f":"Blue, yellow and red with an eagle holding a cross. It looks like Romania's flag because the two countries share a history and language."},{"c":"MG","n":"Madagascar","cap":"Antananarivo","ct":"Africa","sub":"Eastern Africa","ll":[-20,47],"id":"450","t":5,"f":"A white vertical band next to red over green. White stands for purity, red for independence, and green for hope and the people of the coast."},{"c":"MV","n":"Maldives","cap":"Malé","ct":"Asia","sub":"Southern Asia","ll":[3.25,73],"id":"462","t":4,"f":"A green rectangle with a white crescent on red. Red stands for the sacrifices of national heroes, green for peace and the islands' palm trees, and the crescent for Islam."},{"c":"MX","n":"Mexico","cap":"Mexico City","ct":"North America","sub":"North America","ll":[23,-102],"id":"484","t":1,"f":"Green, white and red with an eagle on a cactus eating a snake. It comes from an Aztec legend: the gods told them to build their city where they saw this sight — which became Tenochtitlan, today's Mexico City."},{"c":"MH","n":"Marshall Islands","cap":"Majuro","ct":"Oceania","sub":"Micronesia","ll":[9,168],"id":"584","t":5,"f":"Blue with a white star and two rays, orange and white, getting wider. The rays stand for the equator (orange for courage, white for peace), and the star's 24 points stand for the country's electoral districts."},{"c":"MK","n":"North Macedonia","cap":"Skopje","ct":"Europe","sub":"Southeast Europe","ll":[41.83,22],"id":"807","t":4,"f":"A yellow sun with eight widening rays on red. It stands for 'the new sun of liberty' mentioned in the national anthem."},{"c":"ML","n":"Mali","cap":"Bamako","ct":"Africa","sub":"Western Africa","ll":[17,-4],"id":"466","t":4,"f":"Green, gold and red vertical stripes, the Pan-African colours. It's Guinea's flag in reverse order."},{"c":"MT","n":"Malta","cap":"Valletta","ct":"Europe","sub":"Southern Europe","ll":[35.83,14.58],"id":"470","t":3,"f":"White and red with the George Cross in the corner. King George VI awarded the George Cross to the whole people of Malta in 1942 for their bravery during a long siege in World War II."},{"c":"MM","n":"Myanmar","cap":"Naypyidaw","ct":"Asia","sub":"South-Eastern Asia","ll":[22,98],"id":"104","t":4,"f":"Yellow, green and red stripes with a large white star. Yellow stands for unity, green for peace and the land, red for courage, and the star for a united country."},{"c":"ME","n":"Montenegro","cap":"Podgorica","ct":"Europe","sub":"Southeast Europe","ll":[42.5,19.3],"id":"499","t":4,"f":"Red with a gold border and a golden double-headed eagle. The eagle comes from Montenegro's royal history."},{"c":"MN","n":"Mongolia","cap":"Ulan Bator","ct":"Asia","sub":"Eastern Asia","ll":[46,105],"id":"496","t":3,"f":"Red, blue and red with a golden Soyombo symbol. Blue is Mongolia's 'eternal blue sky', and the Soyombo's shapes stand for fire, the sun, the moon and the earth."},{"c":"MZ","n":"Mozambique","cap":"Maputo","ct":"Africa","sub":"Eastern Africa","ll":[-18.25,35],"id":"508","t":4,"f":"Green, black and yellow stripes with a red triangle holding a star, a book, a hoe and an AK-47 rifle. The book stands for education, the hoe for farming, and the rifle for defence — it's the only national flag with a modern rifle."},{"c":"MR","n":"Mauritania","cap":"Nouakchott","ct":"Africa","sub":"Western Africa","ll":[20,-12],"id":"478","t":5,"f":"Green with a gold crescent and star, and red stripes at the top and bottom. The crescent and star stand for Islam; the red stripes were added in 2017 to honour those who died for independence."},{"c":"MU","n":"Mauritius","cap":"Port Louis","ct":"Africa","sub":"Eastern Africa","ll":[-20.28,57.55],"id":"480","t":5,"f":"Four stripes — red, blue, yellow and green. Red stands for the struggle for independence, blue for the Indian Ocean, yellow for the light of independence, and green for farming."},{"c":"MW","n":"Malawi","cap":"Lilongwe","ct":"Africa","sub":"Eastern Africa","ll":[-13.5,34],"id":"454","t":5,"f":"Black, red and green stripes with a red rising sun. The rising sun stands for the dawn of hope and freedom for Africa."},{"c":"MY","n":"Malaysia","cap":"Kuala Lumpur","ct":"Asia","sub":"South-Eastern Asia","ll":[2.5,112.5],"id":"458","t":3,"f":"Fourteen red and white stripes and a yellow crescent and 14-pointed star on blue, called 'Jalur Gemilang' (Stripes of Glory). The 14 stand for Malaysia's 13 states plus the federal government."},{"c":"NA","n":"Namibia","cap":"Windhoek","ct":"Africa","sub":"Southern Africa","ll":[-22,17],"id":"516","t":5,"f":"A red diagonal band edged in white, with blue and green triangles and a golden sun. Blue is the sky and ocean, red the people's courage, green the plants, and the sun stands for life and energy."},{"c":"NE","n":"Niger","cap":"Niamey","ct":"Africa","sub":"Western Africa","ll":[16,8],"id":"562","t":5,"f":"Orange, white and green stripes with an orange circle. Orange stands for the Sahara Desert, white for purity, green for the fertile south and the Niger River, and the circle for the sun. It looks a bit like India's flag!"},{"c":"NG","n":"Nigeria","cap":"Abuja","ct":"Africa","sub":"Western Africa","ll":[10,8],"id":"566","t":2,"f":"Green, white and green vertical stripes. Green stands for the country's forests and farmland and white for peace. It was designed in 1959 by a student, Michael Taiwo Akinkunmi."},{"c":"NI","n":"Nicaragua","cap":"Managua","ct":"North America","sub":"Central America","ll":[13,-85],"id":"558","t":5,"f":"Blue, white and blue with a coat of arms: a triangle with five volcanoes and a rainbow. The rainbow makes it one of only two national flags that use purple."},{"c":"NL","n":"Netherlands","cap":"Amsterdam","ct":"Europe","sub":"Western Europe","ll":[52.5,5.75],"id":"528","t":2,"f":"Red, white and blue stripes. It comes from the orange, white and blue 'Prince's Flag' of William of Orange, making it one of the oldest tricolour flags in the world."},{"c":"NO","n":"Norway","cap":"Oslo","ct":"Europe","sub":"Northern Europe","ll":[62,10],"id":"578","t":2,"f":"A blue cross outlined in white on red. The red, white and blue were chosen as colours of freedom, inspired by the flags of France, the United States and the United Kingdom."},{"c":"NP","n":"Nepal","cap":"Kathmandu","ct":"Asia","sub":"Southern Asia","ll":[28,84],"id":"524","t":2,"f":"Two stacked crimson triangles with a moon and sun. It's the only national flag that isn't a rectangle. The two triangles are often said to stand for the Himalaya mountains."},{"c":"NR","n":"Nauru","cap":"Yaren","ct":"Oceania","sub":"Micronesia","ll":[-0.53,166.92],"id":"520","t":5,"f":"Dark blue with a thin yellow line and a white 12-pointed star. The line is the equator and the star is Nauru sitting just below it; the 12 points stand for its 12 original tribes."},{"c":"NZ","n":"New Zealand","cap":"Wellington","ct":"Oceania","sub":"Australia and New Zealand","ll":[-41,174],"id":"554","t":2,"f":"The Union Jack and four red stars with white borders. The stars form the Southern Cross constellation. Don't mix it up with Australia's flag, which has white stars and a big seven-pointed star."},{"c":"OM","n":"Oman","cap":"Muscat","ct":"Asia","sub":"Western Asia","ll":[21,57],"id":"512","t":5,"f":"White, red and green stripes with a red band at the pole bearing the national emblem: a curved khanjar dagger over two crossed swords."},{"c":"PK","n":"Pakistan","cap":"Islamabad","ct":"Asia","sub":"Southern Asia","ll":[30,70],"id":"586","t":2,"f":"A white crescent and star on dark green, with a white band at the pole. Green stands for the Muslim majority and white for religious minorities; the crescent means progress and the star means light and knowledge."},{"c":"PA","n":"Panama","cap":"Panama City","ct":"North America","sub":"Central America","ll":[9,-80],"id":"591","t":3,"f":"Four squares: white with a blue star, red, blue, and white with a red star. Blue and red stood for Panama's two old political parties, and white for peace between them."},{"c":"PE","n":"Peru","cap":"Lima","ct":"South America","sub":"South America","ll":[-10,-76],"id":"604","t":2,"f":"Red, white and red vertical stripes. Legend says General José de San Martín was inspired by flamingos with red wings and white chests that he saw on the coast."},{"c":"PH","n":"Philippines","cap":"Manila","ct":"Asia","sub":"South-Eastern Asia","ll":[13,122],"id":"608","t":2,"f":"Blue over red with a white triangle, a golden sun with eight rays and three stars. The rays stand for the first eight provinces to revolt against Spain, and the stars for the main island groups: Luzon, Visayas and Mindanao."},{"c":"PW","n":"Palau","cap":"Ngerulmud","ct":"Oceania","sub":"Micronesia","ll":[7.5,134.5],"id":"585","t":5,"f":"A yellow disc on light blue, slightly towards the pole. It's the full moon over the Pacific — in Palauan tradition the full moon is the best time for fishing, planting and celebrating."},{"c":"PG","n":"Papua New Guinea","cap":"Port Moresby","ct":"Oceania","sub":"Melanesia","ll":[-6,147],"id":"598","t":4,"f":"A diagonal split with a golden bird-of-paradise on red and the Southern Cross on black. It was designed in 1971 by a 15-year-old schoolgirl, Susan Karike."},{"c":"PL","n":"Poland","cap":"Warsaw","ct":"Europe","sub":"Central Europe","ll":[52,20],"id":"616","t":2,"f":"White over red, the colours of Poland's coat of arms: a white eagle on red. Upside down, it would look like the flags of Indonesia and Monaco."},{"c":"KP","n":"North Korea","cap":"Pyongyang","ct":"Asia","sub":"Eastern Asia","ll":[40,127],"id":"408","t":3,"f":"Blue, red and blue stripes with thin white edges, and a red star in a white circle. Red stands for revolution, white for purity, and blue for the wish for peace."},{"c":"PT","n":"Portugal","cap":"Lisbon","ct":"Europe","sub":"Southern Europe","ll":[39.5,-8],"id":"620","t":2,"f":"Green and red with an armillary sphere and a shield. The armillary sphere was a navigation instrument, a reminder of Portugal's great sea voyages in the Age of Discovery."},{"c":"PY","n":"Paraguay","cap":"Asunción","ct":"South America","sub":"South America","ll":[-23,-58],"id":"600","t":3,"f":"Red, white and blue stripes with an emblem — and a different emblem on each side! The front shows the national coat of arms and the back shows the treasury seal with a lion."},{"c":"QA","n":"Qatar","cap":"Doha","ct":"Asia","sub":"Western Asia","ll":[25.5,51.25],"id":"634","t":4,"f":"Maroon with a white zig-zag band with nine points. It's the only national flag more than twice as wide as it is tall, and the nine points mark Qatar as the ninth member of the 'reconciled emirates' of the Persian Gulf."},{"c":"RO","n":"Romania","cap":"Bucharest","ct":"Europe","sub":"Southeast Europe","ll":[46,25],"id":"642","t":3,"f":"Blue, yellow and red vertical stripes. It's almost identical to Chad's flag — in 2004 Chad asked the United Nations to look into it, but Romania kept its flag."},{"c":"RU","n":"Russia","cap":"Moscow","ct":"Europe","sub":"Eastern Europe","tc":true,"ll":[60,100],"id":"643","t":1,"f":"White, blue and red stripes. The design dates back to the time of Tsar Peter the Great and was brought back when the Soviet Union ended in 1991."},{"c":"RW","n":"Rwanda","cap":"Kigali","ct":"Africa","sub":"Eastern Africa","ll":[-2,30],"id":"646","t":3,"f":"Sky blue, yellow and green stripes with a golden sun. Adopted in 2001, blue stands for happiness and peace, yellow for economic growth, green for prosperity, and the sun for the light of a new start."},{"c":"SA","n":"Saudi Arabia","cap":"Riyadh","ct":"Asia","sub":"Western Asia","ll":[25,45],"id":"682","t":2,"f":"Green with white Arabic writing and a sword. The writing is the Shahada, the Islamic declaration of faith. Because it's sacred, the flag is never flown at half-mast and is made so the text reads correctly from both sides."},{"c":"SD","n":"Sudan","cap":"Khartoum","ct":"Africa","sub":"Northern Africa","ll":[15,30],"id":"729","t":4,"f":"Red, white and black stripes with a green triangle at the pole, using the Pan-Arab colours shared by many Arab nations."},{"c":"SN","n":"Senegal","cap":"Dakar","ct":"Africa","sub":"Western Africa","ll":[14,-14],"id":"686","t":4,"f":"Green, yellow and red vertical stripes with a green star. The colours are the Pan-African colours, and the star stands for unity and hope."},{"c":"SG","n":"Singapore","cap":"Singapore","ct":"Asia","sub":"South-Eastern Asia","ll":[1.37,103.8],"id":"702","t":2,"f":"Red over white with a white crescent and five stars. The crescent stands for a young nation on the rise, and the stars for democracy, peace, progress, justice and equality."},{"c":"SB","n":"Solomon Islands","cap":"Honiara","ct":"Oceania","sub":"Melanesia","ll":[-8,159],"id":"090","t":5,"f":"A yellow diagonal stripe between blue and green triangles, with five white stars. The stars stand for the country's main island groups, blue for the ocean and green for the land."},{"c":"SL","n":"Sierra Leone","cap":"Freetown","ct":"Africa","sub":"Western Africa","ll":[8.5,-11.5],"id":"694","t":5,"f":"Green, white and blue stripes. Green stands for farming and the mountains, white for unity and justice, and blue for the natural harbour of the capital, Freetown."},{"c":"SV","n":"El Salvador","cap":"San Salvador","ct":"North America","sub":"Central America","ll":[13.83,-88.92],"id":"222","t":5,"f":"Blue, white and blue with a coat of arms showing five volcanoes. The five volcanoes stand for the five countries of the old Federal Republic of Central America, and the motto reads 'God, Union, Liberty'."},{"c":"SM","n":"San Marino","cap":"City of San Marino","ct":"Europe","sub":"Southern Europe","ll":[43.77,12.42],"id":"674","t":5,"f":"White over light blue with a coat of arms showing three towers on three peaks of Monte Titano. White stands for peace and the snow on the mountain, and blue for the sky."},{"c":"SO","n":"Somalia","cap":"Mogadishu","ct":"Africa","sub":"Eastern Africa","ll":[10,49],"id":"706","t":4,"f":"A white five-pointed star on light blue. The five points stand for the five regions where Somali people live, and the blue was first chosen to honour the United Nations."},{"c":"RS","n":"Serbia","cap":"Belgrade","ct":"Europe","sub":"Southeast Europe","ll":[44,21],"id":"688","t":4,"f":"Red, blue and white stripes with a double-headed white eagle. The eagle has been a Serbian symbol since the Middle Ages."},{"c":"SS","n":"South Sudan","cap":"Juba","ct":"Africa","sub":"Middle Africa","ll":[7,30],"id":"728","t":5,"f":"Black, red and green stripes edged in white, with a blue triangle and a gold star. South Sudan became the world's newest country in 2011; the star stands for the unity of its states."},{"c":"ST","n":"São Tomé and Príncipe","cap":"São Tomé","ct":"Africa","sub":"Middle Africa","ll":[1,7],"id":"678","t":5,"f":"Green, yellow and green stripes with a red triangle and two black stars. The two stars stand for the country's two main islands, São Tomé and Príncipe."},{"c":"SR","n":"Suriname","cap":"Paramaribo","ct":"South America","sub":"South America","ll":[4,-56],"id":"740","t":5,"f":"Green, white, red, white and green stripes with a yellow star. The star stands for the unity of all of Suriname's many ethnic groups."},{"c":"SK","n":"Slovakia","cap":"Bratislava","ct":"Europe","sub":"Central Europe","ll":[48.67,19.5],"id":"703","t":4,"f":"White, blue and red with a white double cross on three blue hills. The hills stand for three mountain ranges, and the double cross for Christianity."},{"c":"SI","n":"Slovenia","cap":"Ljubljana","ct":"Europe","sub":"Central Europe","ll":[46.12,14.82],"id":"705","t":4,"f":"White, blue and red with a shield showing Mount Triglav, Slovenia's highest peak, above wavy lines for its seas and rivers, and three golden stars."},{"c":"SE","n":"Sweden","cap":"Stockholm","ct":"Europe","sub":"Northern Europe","ll":[62,15],"id":"752","t":1,"f":"A yellow cross on blue. The colours come from Sweden's coat of arms, which shows three golden crowns on a blue background."},{"c":"SZ","n":"Eswatini","cap":"Lobamba","ct":"Africa","sub":"Southern Africa","ll":[-26.5,31.5],"id":"748","t":5,"f":"Blue, yellow and crimson stripes with a black-and-white Nguni shield and two spears. The shield and spears stand for protection of the country."},{"c":"SC","n":"Seychelles","cap":"Victoria","ct":"Africa","sub":"Eastern Africa","ll":[-4.58,55.67],"id":"690","t":5,"f":"Five slanting bands of blue, yellow, red, white and green that fan out from the bottom corner, showing a young country moving into the future."},{"c":"SY","n":"Syria","cap":"Damascus","ct":"Asia","sub":"Western Asia","ll":[35,38],"id":"760","t":4,"f":"Green, white and black stripes with three red stars. This 'independence flag' was first used in 1932 under French rule and was adopted again after the fall of the Assad government in December 2024."},{"c":"TD","n":"Chad","cap":"N'Djamena","ct":"Africa","sub":"Middle Africa","ll":[15,19],"id":"148","t":5,"f":"Blue, yellow and red vertical stripes — almost identical to Romania's flag! Chad's blue is just a little darker. Blue stands for the sky and hope, yellow for the sun and desert, and red for the country's sacrifices."},{"c":"TG","n":"Togo","cap":"Lomé","ct":"Africa","sub":"Western Africa","ll":[8,1.17],"id":"768","t":5,"f":"Five green and yellow stripes with a white star on a red square. The five stripes stand for Togo's five regions."},{"c":"TH","n":"Thailand","cap":"Bangkok","ct":"Asia","sub":"South-Eastern Asia","ll":[15,100],"id":"764","t":2,"f":"Red, white, blue, white and red stripes, called the 'Trairanga'. Red stands for the nation, white for religion (Buddhism) and blue for the monarchy."},{"c":"TJ","n":"Tajikistan","cap":"Dushanbe","ct":"Asia","sub":"Central Asia","ll":[39,71],"id":"762","t":5,"f":"Red, white and green stripes with a golden crown under an arc of seven stars. Red stands for unity, white for the cotton harvest and purity, and green for Tajikistan's valleys."},{"c":"TM","n":"Turkmenistan","cap":"Ashgabat","ct":"Asia","sub":"Central Asia","ll":[40,60],"id":"795","t":5,"f":"Green with a red vertical stripe holding five traditional carpet patterns ('guls'), olive branches, a white crescent and five stars. The five carpet designs stand for the country's five main tribes."},{"c":"TL","n":"Timor-Leste","cap":"Dili","ct":"Asia","sub":"South-Eastern Asia","ll":[-8.83,125.92],"id":"626","t":5,"f":"Red with a black triangle over a yellow triangle, and a white star. The star is a guiding light to peace, black stands for the past, and yellow for the traces of colonialism."},{"c":"TO","n":"Tonga","cap":"Nuku'alofa","ct":"Oceania","sub":"Polynesia","ll":[-20,-175],"id":"776","t":5,"f":"Red with a white corner holding a red cross, a symbol of Christianity. Tonga's constitution says the flag must never be changed."},{"c":"TT","n":"Trinidad and Tobago","cap":"Port of Spain","ct":"North America","sub":"Caribbean","ll":[11,-61],"id":"780","t":4,"f":"Red with a black diagonal band edged in white. Red stands for the sun's energy and the people, black for strength, and white for the sea."},{"c":"TN","n":"Tunisia","cap":"Tunis","ct":"Africa","sub":"Northern Africa","ll":[34,9],"id":"788","t":3,"f":"Red with a red crescent and star in a white circle. The design is based on the flag of the Ottoman Empire."},{"c":"TR","n":"Türkiye","cap":"Ankara","ct":"Asia","sub":"Western Asia","tc":true,"ll":[39,35],"id":"792","t":1,"f":"A white crescent and star on red. Legend says the design came from the reflection of the moon and a star in pools of blood after a battle. It's one of the most recognisable flags in the world."},{"c":"TV","n":"Tuvalu","cap":"Funafuti","ct":"Oceania","sub":"Polynesia","ll":[-8,178],"id":"798","t":5,"f":"Light blue with the Union Jack and nine gold stars. The stars are placed like Tuvalu's nine islands on a map."},{"c":"TZ","n":"Tanzania","cap":"Dodoma","ct":"Africa","sub":"Eastern Africa","ll":[-6,35],"id":"834","t":3,"f":"A black diagonal band edged in gold, with green and blue triangles. Green stands for the land, blue for the Indian Ocean and lakes, black for the people, and gold for mineral wealth."},{"c":"UG","n":"Uganda","cap":"Kampala","ct":"Africa","sub":"Eastern Africa","ll":[1,32],"id":"800","t":3,"f":"Black, yellow and red stripes with a grey crowned crane, the national bird, standing on one leg in a white circle."},{"c":"UA","n":"Ukraine","cap":"Kyiv","ct":"Europe","sub":"Eastern Europe","ll":[49,32],"id":"804","t":1,"f":"Blue over yellow — often said to show a blue sky above golden fields of wheat."},{"c":"UY","n":"Uruguay","cap":"Montevideo","ct":"South America","sub":"South America","ll":[-33,-56],"id":"858","t":3,"f":"Nine white and blue stripes with a golden 'Sun of May' — the same sun as on Argentina's flag. The nine stripes stand for Uruguay's nine original regions."},{"c":"US","n":"United States","cap":"Washington D.C.","ct":"North America","sub":"North America","ll":[38,-97],"id":"840","t":1,"f":"Nicknamed 'the Stars and Stripes': 50 white stars for the 50 states and 13 stripes for the 13 original colonies. This version has been used since 4 July 1960, after Hawaii became the 50th state."},{"c":"UZ","n":"Uzbekistan","cap":"Tashkent","ct":"Asia","sub":"Central Asia","ll":[41,64],"id":"860","t":4,"f":"Blue, white and green stripes with thin red lines, a white crescent and 12 stars. The crescent stands for the new independent country and the 12 stars for the months of the year."},{"c":"VA","n":"Vatican City","cap":"Vatican City","ct":"Europe","sub":"Southern Europe","ll":[41.9,12.45],"id":"336","t":4,"f":"Yellow and white with the crossed keys of Saint Peter and the Pope's crown. Like Switzerland's, it's one of only two square national flags."},{"c":"VC","n":"Saint Vincent and the Grenadines","cap":"Kingstown","ct":"North America","sub":"Caribbean","ll":[13.25,-61.2],"id":"670","t":5,"f":"Blue, gold and green stripes with three green diamonds in a V shape. The diamonds stand for the islands, nicknamed 'the Gems of the Antilles'."},{"c":"VE","n":"Venezuela","cap":"Caracas","ct":"South America","sub":"South America","ll":[8,-66],"id":"862","t":3,"f":"Yellow, blue and red stripes with an arc of eight white stars. The stars stand for the provinces that signed the 1811 declaration of independence; the eighth was added in 2006."},{"c":"VN","n":"Vietnam","cap":"Hanoi","ct":"Asia","sub":"South-Eastern Asia","ll":[16.17,107.83],"id":"704","t":2,"f":"A large yellow star on red. The star's five points stand for Vietnam's workers, farmers, soldiers, scholars and young people."},{"c":"VU","n":"Vanuatu","cap":"Port Vila","ct":"Oceania","sub":"Melanesia","ll":[-16,167],"id":"548","t":5,"f":"Red and green halves with a black triangle and a yellow Y shape. On the triangle is a curled boar's tusk (a symbol of wealth) and two leaves of the namele fern (a symbol of peace)."},{"c":"WS","n":"Samoa","cap":"Apia","ct":"Oceania","sub":"Polynesia","ll":[-13.58,-172.33],"id":"882","t":5,"f":"Red with a blue corner holding five white stars in the shape of the Southern Cross constellation."},{"c":"YE","n":"Yemen","cap":"Sana'a","ct":"Asia","sub":"Western Asia","ll":[15,48],"id":"887","t":4,"f":"Red, white and black stripes — the Pan-Arab colours. Red stands for the blood of martyrs, white for a bright future, and black for the dark past."},{"c":"ZA","n":"South Africa","cap":null,"ct":"Africa","sub":"Southern Africa","ll":[-29,24],"id":"710","t":2,"f":"A green 'Y' shape with red, blue, black, gold and white. Adopted in 1994 as apartheid ended, the Y shows different parts of South African society coming together and moving forward as one."},{"c":"ZM","n":"Zambia","cap":"Lusaka","ct":"Africa","sub":"Eastern Africa","ll":[-15,30],"id":"894","t":5,"f":"Green with an orange eagle in flight above red, black and orange stripes. The eagle stands for the country's ability to rise above its problems; orange is for copper, Zambia's biggest export."},{"c":"ZW","n":"Zimbabwe","cap":"Harare","ct":"Africa","sub":"Eastern Africa","ll":[-20,30],"id":"716","t":3,"f":"Green, yellow, red and black stripes with a white triangle, a red star and the Zimbabwe Bird. The bird is a carving found at the ancient city of Great Zimbabwe."}],"lookalike":[["TD","RO","AD","MD","BE"],["ID","MC","PL","SG"],["IE","CI","IT"],["NL","LU","RU","FR","PY","HR","SK","SI","RS"],["AU","NZ","FJ","TV"],["NO","IS","DK","FI","SE"],["ML","GN","SN","CM","GH","BO","LT","ET"],["CO","EC","VE"],["HN","NI","SV","GT","AR"],["EG","SY","IQ","YE","SD"],["JO","SD","KW","AE"],["TR","TN","AZ","PK","DZ"],["QA","BH"],["CU","PH","CZ"],["BE","DE"],["AT","LV","LB","PE"],["JP","BD","PW","LA"],["US","LR","MY"],["HT","LI"],["HU","IT","IR","TJ","BG"],["NG","IE"],["NE","IN"],["CL","CZ"],["UA","SE"],["GH","BO","LT"],["CN","VN"],["KR","JP"],["SA","PK"],["MX","IT"],["UY","AR","GR"],["TT","KN"],["MY","US","LR"],["BR","PT"]],"curated":[{"g":3,"img":"US","q":"How many stars are on the flag of the United States?","c":["13","48","50","52"],"a":2,"why":"One star for each of the 50 states. The current flag has been flown since 4 July 1960, after Hawaii became the 50th state."},{"g":3,"img":"US","q":"How many stripes are on the flag of the United States?","c":["10","13","15","50"],"a":1,"why":"13 red and white stripes stand for the 13 original British colonies that declared independence in 1776."},{"g":3,"img":"CA","q":"What kind of leaf is on Canada's flag?","c":["An oak leaf","A maple leaf","A palm leaf","A clover leaf"],"a":1,"why":"Maple trees grow all across Canada, and the maple leaf has been a Canadian symbol for more than 200 years. Canada is also famous for maple syrup!"},{"g":3,"img":"JP","q":"What does the red circle on Japan's flag stand for?","c":["The sun","An apple","The moon","A rose"],"a":0,"why":"It's the sun. Japan's name, Nippon, means 'origin of the sun', which is why it's called the Land of the Rising Sun."},{"g":3,"img":"MX","q":"Which bird is in the middle of Mexico's flag?","c":["An eagle","A parrot","A flamingo","An owl"],"a":0,"why":"An eagle perched on a cactus, eating a snake. According to Aztec legend, the gods told the Aztecs to build their city where they saw this — and that city became Mexico City."},{"g":3,"img":"GB","q":"What is the nickname of the United Kingdom's flag?","c":["The Stars and Stripes","The Tricolore","The Union Jack","The Maple Leaf"],"a":2,"why":"It's called the Union Jack (or Union Flag) because it unites the crosses of England, Scotland and Ireland."},{"g":3,"img":"FR","q":"What colours are on the flag of France?","c":["Blue, white and red","Green, white and red","Red, yellow and black","Blue and yellow"],"a":0,"why":"France's 'Tricolore' is blue, white and red. It became the national flag during the French Revolution."},{"g":3,"img":"US","q":"Which country's flag is nicknamed 'the Stars and Stripes'?","c":["Liberia","Chile","The United States","Malaysia"],"a":2,"why":"The US flag is called 'the Stars and Stripes'. Liberia and Malaysia have stars and stripes too, but the nickname belongs to the United States."},{"g":3,"img":"VN","q":"What colour is the star on Vietnam's flag?","c":["White","Yellow","Blue","Green"],"a":1,"why":"A big yellow (gold) star sits on a red background. Its five points stand for workers, farmers, soldiers, scholars and young people."},{"g":3,"after":"CH","q":"Which country's flag is a red square with a white cross in the middle?","c":["Canada","Switzerland","Japan","Türkiye"],"a":1,"why":"Switzerland's flag is square, with a white cross on red. Denmark also has a white cross on red, but its flag is a rectangle and the cross is off-centre."},{"g":3,"img":"BR","q":"What is shown inside the blue circle on Brazil's flag?","c":["A football","Stars in the night sky","The sun","A map of Brazil"],"a":1,"why":"The blue globe shows the stars in the sky over Rio de Janeiro on 15 November 1889, the day Brazil became a republic."},{"g":4,"img":"AU","q":"Which of these is NOT on Australia's flag?","c":["The Union Jack","The Southern Cross","A kangaroo","The Commonwealth Star"],"a":2,"why":"There's no kangaroo! Australia's flag has the Union Jack, the seven-pointed Commonwealth Star and the Southern Cross constellation."},{"g":4,"img":"UA","q":"Ukraine's blue and yellow flag is often said to show…","c":["Blue sky above golden wheat fields","The sea above a sandy beach","Water and the sun","Night and day"],"a":0,"why":"Blue sky over golden fields of wheat — Ukraine is one of the world's great grain-growing countries."},{"g":4,"img":"MX","q":"On Mexico's flag, what is the eagle eating?","c":["A fish","A snake","A mouse","A cactus fruit"],"a":1,"why":"A snake. The Aztecs were told to build their city where they found an eagle on a cactus devouring a snake."},{"g":4,"after":"BT","q":"Which country's flag shows a dragon?","c":["China","Bhutan","Japan","Thailand"],"a":1,"why":"Bhutan's flag shows Druk, the white Thunder Dragon. Bhutan calls itself Druk Yul, 'Land of the Thunder Dragon'."},{"g":4,"img":"CN","q":"How many stars are on China's flag?","c":["One","Three","Five","Seven"],"a":2,"why":"Five yellow stars: one large star for the Communist Party and four smaller stars for the people."},{"g":4,"after":"AU","q":"Which flag shows the Southern Cross, a group of stars seen in the southern sky?","c":["Canada","Norway","Australia","Japan"],"a":2,"why":"Australia's flag shows the Southern Cross. New Zealand, Brazil, Samoa and Papua New Guinea show it too — they're all in the Southern Hemisphere."},{"g":4,"img":"FI","q":"What colour is the cross on Finland's flag?","c":["Red","Yellow","Blue","Green"],"a":2,"why":"Blue — for Finland's thousands of lakes and its sky — on white, for its snowy winters."},{"g":4,"img":"LB","q":"What kind of tree is on Lebanon's flag?","c":["A palm tree","An oak tree","A cedar tree","A maple tree"],"a":2,"why":"The cedar of Lebanon. These ancient trees are a symbol of strength and eternity and are even mentioned in the Bible."},{"g":5,"after":"TD","q":"Which country's flag is almost identical to Romania's?","c":["Chad","Belgium","Ireland","Germany"],"a":0,"why":"Chad and Romania both have blue, yellow and red vertical stripes. Chad's blue is just a little darker."},{"g":5,"after":"NP","q":"Which country has the only national flag that is NOT a rectangle?","c":["Switzerland","Nepal","Qatar","Bhutan"],"a":1,"why":"Nepal's flag is made of two stacked triangles, often said to stand for the Himalaya mountains."},{"g":5,"img":"US","q":"What do the 13 stripes on the US flag stand for?","c":["The first 13 presidents","The 13 original colonies","13 famous battles","The 13 largest states"],"a":1,"why":"The 13 British colonies that declared independence in 1776 and became the first states."},{"g":5,"after":"JM","q":"Which of these flags uses NO red, white or blue?","c":["France","Japan","Jamaica","Chile"],"a":2,"why":"Jamaica's flag is black, green and gold. It's the only national flag with none of red, white or blue."},{"g":5,"after":"SE","q":"Which of these flags does NOT have a Nordic (Scandinavian) cross?","c":["Sweden","Finland","Netherlands","Iceland"],"a":2,"why":"The Netherlands has three horizontal stripes. Sweden, Finland and Iceland all have the off-centre 'Nordic cross' that started with Denmark's flag."},{"g":5,"img":"IN","q":"What is the wheel on India's flag called?","c":["The Ashoka Chakra","The Sun of May","The Taegeuk","The Soyombo"],"a":0,"why":"The Ashoka Chakra comes from a lion carving made for Emperor Ashoka over 2,200 years ago. It stands for the 'wheel of law'."},{"g":5,"img":"BR","q":"What does Brazil's motto 'Ordem e Progresso' mean?","c":["Freedom or Death","Order and Progress","God and Country","Unity is Strength"],"a":1,"why":"'Ordem e Progresso' is Portuguese for 'Order and Progress'. It's written on the white band across the blue globe."},{"g":6,"after":"CH","q":"Which two countries have square national flags?","c":["Switzerland and Vatican City","Nepal and Bhutan","Japan and South Korea","Chad and Romania"],"a":0,"why":"Switzerland and Vatican City are the only two countries with square flags."},{"g":6,"after":"CY","q":"Which country's flag shows a map of the country?","c":["Canada","Cyprus","Japan","Peru"],"a":1,"why":"Cyprus shows its island in a copper colour, because the island was famous for copper mining in ancient times."},{"g":6,"after":"KH","q":"Which country's flag shows a famous temple?","c":["Thailand","Cambodia","India","Greece"],"a":1,"why":"Cambodia's flag shows Angkor Wat, a giant temple built in the 12th century — one of the largest religious buildings in the world."},{"g":6,"after":"AR","q":"The golden 'Sun of May' appears on the flags of which two countries?","c":["Argentina and Uruguay","Peru and Chile","Japan and South Korea","Brazil and Portugal"],"a":0,"why":"Argentina and Uruguay both show the Sun of May, a symbol of the May Revolution of 1810 against Spanish rule."},{"g":6,"after":"DK","q":"Which flag is said to be the oldest national flag still in use?","c":["Sweden's","Norway's","Denmark's","Finland's"],"a":2,"why":"Denmark's 'Dannebrog'. Legend says it fell from the sky during a battle in 1219!"},{"g":6,"img":"IE","q":"On Ireland's flag, what does the white stripe stand for?","c":["Snow","Peace between two communities","The Atlantic Ocean","The Irish harp"],"a":1,"why":"Green stands for the Gaelic tradition, orange for supporters of William of Orange, and white for peace between them."},{"g":6,"after":"AL","q":"Which country's flag shows a black double-headed eagle?","c":["Albania","Poland","Germany","Egypt"],"a":0,"why":"Albania's eagle was the symbol of the national hero Skanderbeg. Poland's eagle is white with one head, and Egypt's is golden."},{"g":6,"after":"ET","q":"Green, yellow and red are called the 'Pan-African colours'. Which country's flag inspired them?","c":["Ethiopia","Ghana","Kenya","Egypt"],"a":0,"why":"Ethiopia's flag inspired them, because Ethiopia was never colonised (apart from a brief Italian occupation, 1936–41). Many newly independent African countries chose these colours."},{"g":6,"img":"UG","q":"Which bird stands on one leg on Uganda's flag?","c":["A flamingo","A grey crowned crane","A bald eagle","A peacock"],"a":1,"why":"The grey crowned crane is Uganda's national bird. It's shown with one leg raised, as if moving forward."},{"g":6,"img":"NZ","q":"How many stars are on New Zealand's flag?","c":["Four","Five","Six","Seven"],"a":0,"why":"Four red stars with white edges form the Southern Cross. Australia's flag has white stars instead — six of them."},{"g":6,"after":"MC","q":"Which country's flag is almost the same as Indonesia's red-over-white flag?","c":["Japan","Austria","Monaco","Singapore"],"a":2,"why":"Monaco's flag is also red over white; only the shape is different. Singapore's is red over white too, but it has a crescent and stars."},{"g":6,"img":"PG","q":"Which bird is on Papua New Guinea's flag?","c":["A kiwi","A bird-of-paradise","A toucan","A parrot"],"a":1,"why":"A golden bird-of-paradise, famous for its colourful feathers, flies across the red half of the flag."},{"g":6,"after":"MA","q":"Which country's flag is red with a green five-pointed star outline?","c":["Vietnam","Morocco","Ghana","China"],"a":1,"why":"Morocco's green star outline is called the Seal of Solomon."},{"g":6,"img":"KI","q":"On Kiribati's flag, a frigatebird flies over…","c":["A snowy mountain","The sun rising over the ocean","A palm tree","A volcano"],"a":1,"why":"A golden sun rising from the Pacific Ocean — Kiribati is one of the first countries to see each new day."},{"g":6,"img":"GB","q":"The Union Jack combines the crosses of which patron saints?","c":["George, Andrew and Patrick","George, David and Andrew","Patrick, David and George","Peter, Paul and George"],"a":0,"why":"Saint George (England), Saint Andrew (Scotland) and Saint Patrick (Ireland)."},{"g":7,"img":"IN","q":"How many spokes does the Ashoka Chakra on India's flag have?","c":["12","16","24","32"],"a":2,"why":"24 spokes. They're often said to stand for the 24 hours of the day."},{"g":7,"img":"KR","q":"What is the red-and-blue circle on South Korea's flag called?","c":["The Taegeuk","The Hinomaru","The Soyombo","The Chakra"],"a":0,"why":"The Taegeuk is a yin-yang symbol for the balance of opposites, like light and dark. The flag itself is called the Taegukgi."},{"g":7,"after":"MZ","q":"Which country's flag shows an AK-47 rifle?","c":["Mozambique","Angola","Zimbabwe","Kenya"],"a":0,"why":"Mozambique's flag shows a rifle (defence), a hoe (farming) and a book (education). It's the only national flag with a modern rifle."},{"g":7,"img":"LI","q":"Why does Liechtenstein's flag have a golden crown?","c":["To celebrate a new prince","Its flag turned out to be identical to Haiti's","The crown is made of real gold","It won a design contest"],"a":1,"why":"At the 1936 Olympics, Liechtenstein discovered its blue-and-red flag was identical to Haiti's, so it added a crown in 1937."},{"g":7,"img":"BR","q":"The 27 stars on Brazil's flag stand for…","c":["Brazil's states and the Federal District","Famous football players","The months of three years","Brazil's biggest cities"],"a":0,"why":"Brazil has 26 states plus the Federal District around its capital, Brasília — one star each."},{"g":7,"img":"LR","q":"Why does Liberia's flag look like the US flag?","c":["Liberia was once a US state","Liberia was founded by freed African-Americans","The same artist designed both","It copied it for the Olympics"],"a":1,"why":"Liberia was founded in the 1820s by freed African-Americans. Its 11 stripes stand for the 11 signers of its Declaration of Independence."},{"g":7,"img":"HT","q":"Haiti's flag began in 1803 when revolutionaries did what to the French flag?","c":["Tore out the white stripe","Added a golden star","Turned it upside down","Painted it green"],"a":0,"why":"They tore out the white stripe, leaving blue and red, to show that Haiti was free from France. Haiti became the first independent Black republic in 1804."},{"g":7,"img":"PY","q":"What is unusual about Paraguay's flag?","c":["It's triangular","It has a different emblem on each side","It changes colour at night","It has no stripes"],"a":1,"why":"The front shows the national coat of arms and the back shows the treasury seal with a lion."},{"g":7,"after":"DM","q":"Which country's flag shows a parrot?","c":["Brazil","Dominica","Mexico","Peru"],"a":1,"why":"Dominica's flag shows the Sisserou parrot, found only on that island. Its purple feathers make Dominica one of only two national flags with purple."},{"g":7,"img":"FM","q":"What do the four stars on Micronesia's flag stand for?","c":["Its four seasons","Its four states","Four famous sailors","Four nearby countries"],"a":1,"why":"One star for each state: Chuuk, Kosrae, Pohnpei and Yap."},{"g":7,"img":"LS","q":"What is in the middle of Lesotho's flag?","c":["A lion","A traditional Basotho hat","A diamond","A shield and spear"],"a":1,"why":"The mokorotlo, a cone-shaped straw hat worn by the Basotho people and a symbol of the nation."},{"g":7,"img":"FR","q":"On France's flag, blue and red were originally the colours of which city?","c":["Lyon","Marseille","Paris","Versailles"],"a":2,"why":"Blue and red were the colours of Paris. They were combined with white, the colour of the French king, during the French Revolution."},{"g":7,"after":"SA","q":"Green, white, black and red are known as the 'Pan-Arab colours'. Which flag does NOT use them?","c":["Jordan","Kuwait","Saudi Arabia","United Arab Emirates"],"a":2,"why":"Saudi Arabia's flag is green and white only. Jordan, Kuwait and the UAE all use red, green, white and black."},{"g":8,"img":"ES","q":"The motto 'Plus Ultra' on Spain's flag means…","c":["Further beyond","Always faithful","Unity is strength","God, Country, King"],"a":0,"why":"'Plus Ultra' is Latin for 'Further beyond'. It celebrates Spain's voyages beyond the Pillars of Hercules (the Strait of Gibraltar) to the Americas."},{"g":8,"img":"GR","q":"Greece's nine stripes are said to stand for the nine syllables of which motto?","c":["Freedom or Death","Unity and Strength","God Save Greece","Olympic Glory"],"a":0,"why":"'Eleftheria i Thanatos' — 'Freedom or Death' — the battle cry of the Greek War of Independence (1821)."},{"g":8,"after":"QA","q":"Which country's flag is more than twice as wide as it is tall?","c":["Nepal","Qatar","Switzerland","Bahrain"],"a":1,"why":"Qatar's flag has a ratio of 11:28, making it the widest national flag."},{"g":8,"after":"DO","q":"Which is the only national flag to show a Bible?","c":["Vatican City","Tonga","Dominican Republic","Portugal"],"a":2,"why":"The Dominican Republic's coat of arms, in the middle of its flag, contains an open Bible."},{"g":8,"img":"GB","q":"Why isn't Wales represented on the Union Jack?","c":["Wales chose not to be","It was already united with England when the first Union Flag was made","Its dragon was too hard to sew","Wales joined later than Ireland"],"a":1,"why":"When the first Union Flag was made in 1606, Wales had already been united with England, so it was counted as part of England."},{"g":8,"img":"NG","q":"Who designed Nigeria's green-white-green flag in 1959?","c":["A famous painter","A student, Michael Taiwo Akinkunmi","The British Queen","A football team"],"a":1,"why":"Michael Taiwo Akinkunmi, a student, won a competition to design the flag. Green is for the land and white for peace."},{"g":8,"img":"PG","q":"Papua New Guinea's flag was designed in 1971 by…","c":["A 15-year-old schoolgirl","The prime minister","An Australian artist","A group of fishermen"],"a":0,"why":"Susan Karike, a 15-year-old student, designed it for a national competition."},{"g":8,"img":"PH","q":"What do the eight rays of the sun on the Philippine flag stand for?","c":["Eight islands","The first eight provinces to revolt against Spain","Eight hours of sunshine","Eight presidents"],"a":1,"why":"They honour the first eight provinces that rose up against Spanish rule in 1896."},{"g":8,"img":"PH","q":"What is special about how the Philippines flies its flag in wartime?","c":["It flies it at night only","It flies it upside down, with red on top","It removes the stars","It flies two flags"],"a":1,"why":"Red (courage) goes on top in wartime; normally blue (peace) is on top. It's one of the only flags that signals war this way."},{"g":8,"img":"SA","q":"Why is Saudi Arabia's flag never flown at half-mast?","c":["It is too heavy","It carries the Islamic declaration of faith","It's always green","The king forbids it"],"a":1,"why":"The Shahada, the Islamic declaration of faith, is written on it, and lowering it is seen as disrespectful to the sacred words."},{"g":8,"img":"TO","q":"What does Tonga's constitution say about its flag?","c":["It must be square","It must never be changed","It must be flown only on Sundays","It must be sewn by the king"],"a":1,"why":"Tonga's constitution states that the flag shall never be altered."},{"g":8,"img":"NR","q":"On Nauru's flag, the thin yellow line stands for…","c":["A sandy beach","The equator","A famous road","The sun's path"],"a":1,"why":"It's the equator — the white star below it shows Nauru's position just south of the equator."},{"g":8,"img":"IR","q":"On Iran's flag, how many times is 'God is great' written along the stripes?","c":["7","11","22","40"],"a":2,"why":"22 times (11 on each edge), marking the 22nd of Bahman, the date in the Iranian calendar when the 1979 revolution succeeded."},{"g":8,"img":"KG","q":"The sun on Kyrgyzstan's flag has 40 rays. What do they stand for?","c":["40 lakes","40 tribes united by the hero Manas","40 years of independence","40 mountains"],"a":1,"why":"In the epic of Manas, the hero united 40 tribes. The shape in the middle is the roof of a yurt."},{"g":8,"img":"MT","q":"Why is there a George Cross on Malta's flag?","c":["Malta is ruled by a King George","It was awarded to the people of Malta for bravery in World War II","It shows the island's shape","It was won at the Olympics"],"a":1,"why":"King George VI awarded the George Cross to all the people of Malta in 1942 for their courage during a long siege."}]};
